{"id": "ind_dc_182a1e726ac1", "answer_text": "Schizophrenia is best modeled as a polygenic, mechanistically heterogeneous neurodevelopmental disorder in which convergent perturbations of synapse formation/maintenance, interneuron-mediated E/I balance, calcium-dependent plasticity, and immune-mediated pruning produce circuit-level dysconnectivity that manifests as cognitive, negative and positive symptoms. Concretely:\n- Complement/C4A: increased C4A expression/activity → excessive complement tagging and microglial pruning of cortical synapses during adolescence → reduced cortical synapse density → cognitive and negative symptoms (confidence: moderate).\n- NMDA/glutamate: NMDA-receptor hypofunction (especially affecting PV interneurons) → cortical disinhibition, impaired gamma oscillations and network instability → cognitive deficits and indirect promotion of subcortical dopamine dysregulation (confidence: moderate).\n- Dopamine/DRD2: altered presynaptic dopamine synthesis/release or D2 signaling in the striatum → aberrant salience and positive psychotic symptoms; pharmacologic D2 antagonism reliably reduces positive symptoms (confidence: high).\n- Synaptic/Developmental genes (CACNA1C, NRXN1, NLGN, SETD1A, DISC1, etc.): variants alter VGCC-mediated excitability, synaptic adhesion, and activity-dependent transcription → impaired synaptogenesis and circuit wiring that increase vulnerability to psychosis (confidence: low–moderate).\nThese branches interact (e.g., synaptic loss and NMDA hypofunction can exacerbate dopamine dysregulation; complement activity can amplify synaptic deficits), and most common-variant effects are small while rare LoF variants can have larger effects in subsets of patients. This synthesis is defensible given current literature consensus but requires formal graph-backed evidence (GWAS fine-mapping, eQTL/TWAS colocalization, cell-type expression, perturbation assays, and imaging correlates). Continued work should run the recommended KG queries and targeted functional experiments to convert this model into a fully graph-supported mechanistic network.", "predicted_genes": [], "predicted_pathways": [], "predicted_phenotypes": [], "predicted_drugs": [], "predicted_relations": [], "bridge_found": false, "mechanism_completeness": null, "graph_faithfulness": null, "utility_score": null, "tool_calls": [], "notes": "experiment=llm_only; max_rounds=3; selected_roles=['GeneCurator', 'PathwayBiologist', 'DiseaseMechanismAnalyst', 'PhenotypeMapper', 'EvidenceReviewer', 'HypothesisIntegrator']"}
